<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ripudaman Singh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1883">1883</a>–<a href="/years/1942">1942</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Maharaja Ripudaman Singh of Nabha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ripudaman Singh was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of Nabha, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> princely state in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, from 1911 to 1923. He supported Sikh religious reform and Indian nationalist causes, which brought him into conflict with the British. After a dispute with neighbouring Patiala he was forced to abdicate in 1923, prompting the Jaito protest, and he died in exile at Kodaikanal in south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ripudaman-singh.jpg" alt="Ripudaman Singh" width="292" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ripudhaman_Singh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
